        "difficulty": "medium",
        "description": "<p data-start=\"23\" data-end=\"134\">As a data analyst for a bustling bookstore chain, you've been tasked with identifying potential bestsellers for an upcoming \"Golden Editions\" promotion. Your manager wants to focus on books with \"gold\" in their title that showed consistent sales in 2025.</p>\n<p data-start=\"23\" data-end=\"134\">You are given two tables, 'books' and 'sales', which record information about book details and their sales.</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"270\">The 'books' table contains the columns 'book_id', 'title', and 'genre', representing each book's identity, title, and genre.</li>\n<li data-start=\"273\" data-end=\"422\">The 'sales' table contains the columns 'sale_id', 'book_id', 'sale_date', and 'copies_sold', representing sales transactions for each book.</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"786\">Write a query to find books whose 'title' contains the word \"gold\" (case-insensitive) and which had sales in the year 2025.</p>\n<p data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"786\">For each qualifying book, calculate</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"786\">The total number of copies sold ('total_sold'),</li>\n<li data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"786\">The average number of copies sold per sale ('avg_copies') and round it to two decimal places.</li>\n<li data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"786\">The number of days between the first and last recorded sale date ('sales_windwow').</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"788\" data-end=\"1036\" data-is-last-node=\"\" data-is-only-node=\"\">The final output should include the columns 'title', 'total_sold', 'avg_copies', and 'sales_windwow', ordered by 'sales_windwow' in descending order, then by 'total_sold' in descending order, and finally by 'title' in ascending order.</p>",
        "schema": "CREATE TABLE books (\n  book_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  title VARCHAR(100),\n  genre VARCHAR(50)\n);\nCREATE TABLE sales (\n  sale_id INT PRIMARY KEY,\n  book_id INT,\n  sale_date DATE,\n  copies_sold INT\n);\nINSERT INTO books VALUES\n  (1, 'Gold Rush Tales',       'History'),\n  (2, 'Mining at Dawn',        'Guide'),\n  (3, 'Golden Age Stories',    'Fiction'),\n  (4, 'Cooking in the West',   'Cooking');\nINSERT INTO sales VALUES\n  (1, 1, '2025-01-10',  20),\n  (2, 1, '2025-02-15',  30),\n  (3, 2, '2025-03-05',  10),\n  (4, 3, '2025-04-12',  25),\n  (5, 3, '2025-05-20',  15),\n  (6, 4, '2025-06-01',  40);\n\n",
